@node	1.3.2, 1.3.3, 1.3.1, 1.3
@subsection Constructing Procedures Using Arrow Functions

In using @code{sum} as in @ref{1.3.1}, it seems terribly awkward to
have to define trivial procedures such as @code{piTerm} and @code{piNext}
just so we can use them as arguments to our higher-order procedure.  Rather
than define @code{piNext} and @code{piTerm}, it would be more convenient to
have a way to directly specify ``the procedure that returns its input
incremented by 4'' and ``the procedure that returns the reciprocal of its input
times its input plus 2.''  We can do this by writing an @newterm{arrow
function}, which creates a procedure without naming it.  Using arrow functions
we can describe what we want as

@example typescript
(x: number): number => x + 4
@end example

@noindent
and

@example typescript
(x: number): number => 1.0 / (x * (x + 2))
@end example

@noindent
Then our @code{piSum} procedure can be expressed without defining any
auxiliary procedures as

@example typescript
const piSumArrow = (a: number, b: number): number =>
  sum(
    (x) => 1.0 / (x * (x + 2)),
    a,
    (x) => x + 4,
    b,
  );
@end example

@noindent
Again using an arrow function, we can write the @code{integral} procedure
without having to define the auxiliary procedure @code{addDx}:

@example typescript
const integralArrow = (
  f: (x: number) => number,
  a: number,
  b: number,
  dx: number,
): number => sum(f, a + dx / 2.0, (x) => x + dx, b) * dx;
@end example

@noindent
In general, an arrow function is used to create a procedure in the same way as
a definition, except that no name is specified for the procedure:

@example typescript
(⟨@var{formal-parameters}⟩) => ⟨@var{body}⟩
@end example

@noindent
The resulting procedure is just as much a procedure as one that is created by a
definition.  The only difference is that it has not been associated with any
name in the environment.  In fact,

@example typescript
function plus4(x: number): number @{
  return x + 4;
@}
@end example

@noindent
is equivalent to

@example typescript
const plus4Arrow = (x: number): number => x + 4;
@end example

@noindent
We can read an arrow function as follows:

@example
(x)                     =>    x + 4
 |                      |     |
the procedure of an argument x that adds x and 4
@end example

@noindent
Like any expression that has a procedure as its value, an arrow function
can be used as the operator in a combination such as

@example typescript
((x: number, y: number, z: number): number =>
  x + y + square(z))(1, 2, 3);
// => 12
@end example

@noindent
or, more generally, in any context where we would normally use a procedure
name.@footnote{The arrow notation is the everyday spelling of the
@math{\lambda}-calculus, a mathematical formalism introduced by the
mathematical logician Alonzo @ref{Church (1941)}.  Church developed the
@math{\lambda}-calculus to provide a rigorous foundation for studying the
notions of function
and function application.  The @math{\lambda}-calculus has become a basic tool
for mathematical investigations of the semantics of programming languages, and
``lambda'' is still the everyday word for the anonymous procedures the arrow
creates.}

@subsubheading Using local bindings to create local variables

Another use of arrow functions is in creating local variables.  We often need
local variables in our procedures other than those that have been bound as
formal parameters.  For example, suppose we wish to compute the function
@displaymath
{f(x,y)} \,=\, {x(1 + xy)^2} + {y(1 - y)} + {(1 + xy)(1 - y),}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
f(x,y) = x(1 + xy)^2 + y(1 - y) + (1 + xy)(1 - y)
@end example

@end ifinfo
@noindent
which we could also express as
@displaymath
\eqalign{
  a                     &=   {1 + xy,}  \cr
  \hphantom{(x,y)} b    &=   {1 - y,}   \cr
  {f(x,y)}              &=   {xa^2} + {yb} + {ab.}
}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
     a = 1 + xy
     b = 1 - y
f(x,y) = xa^2 + yb + ab
@end example

@end ifinfo
In writing a procedure to compute @math{f}, we would like to include as local
variables not only @math{x} and @math{y} but also the names of intermediate
quantities like @math{a} and @math{b}.  One way to accomplish this is to use an
auxiliary procedure to bind the local variables:

@example typescript
const fxy = (x: number, y: number): number => @{
  function fHelper(a: number, b: number): number @{
    return x * square(a) + y * b + a * b;
  @}
  return fHelper(1 + x * y, 1 - y);
@};
@end example

@noindent
Of course, we could use an arrow function to specify an anonymous
procedure for binding our local variables.  The body of @code{fxy} then becomes a
single call to that procedure:

@example typescript
const fxyAnonymous = (x: number, y: number): number =>
  ((a: number, b: number): number =>
    x * square(a) + y * b + a * b)(1 + x * y, 1 - y);
@end example

@noindent
This construct is so useful that the host spells it directly: a block whose
opening lines bind the locals.  Using a block, the @code{fxy} procedure could be written as

@example typescript
const fxyBlock = (x: number, y: number): number => @{
  const a = 1 + x * y;
  const b = 1 - y;
  return x * square(a) + y * b + a * b;
@};
@end example

@noindent
The general form of such a block is

@example typescript
@{
  const ⟨@var{var₁}⟩ = ⟨@var{exp₁}⟩;
  const ⟨@var{var₂}⟩ = ⟨@var{exp₂}⟩;
  @dots{}
  ⟨@var{body}⟩;
@}
@end example

@noindent
which can be thought of as saying

@example
let ⟨@var{var₁}⟩ @r{have the value} ⟨@var{exp₁}⟩ @r{and}
    ⟨@var{var₂}⟩ @r{have the value} ⟨@var{exp₂}⟩ @r{and}
    @r{…}
    ⟨@var{varₙ}⟩ @r{have the value} ⟨@var{expₙ}⟩
  @r{in} ⟨@var{body}⟩
@end example

@noindent
The first lines of the block are a list of name-expression pairs.
When the block is evaluated, each name is associated with the value of the
corresponding expression, computed before the block's own bindings open.  The
body of the block is evaluated with these names bound as local variables.  The
way this happens is exactly what @code{fxyAnonymous} shows: the block is
shorthand for an arrow function applied to the initializer values, with the
initializers evaluated in the surrounding scope.

No new mechanism is required in the language in order to provide local
variables.  A binding block is simply syntactic sugar for the
underlying arrow-function application.

We can see from this equivalence that the scope of a name specified by a
binding block is the body of the block.  This implies that:

@itemize @bullet

@item
A block allows one to bind variables as locally as possible to where they
are to be used.  For example, if the value of @code{x} is 5, the value of the
expression

@example typescript
const x = 5;
((x: number): number => x + x * 10)(3) + x;
// => 38
@end example

@noindent
is 38.  Here, the @code{x} inside the arrow function is 3, so the value of the
call is 33.  On the other hand, the @code{x} that is the second operand of the
outermost @code{+} is still 5.

@item
The variables' values are computed before the block's own bindings open.  This
matters when the expressions that provide the values for the local variables
depend upon variables having the same names as the local variables themselves.
For example, if the value of @code{x} is 2, the expression

@example typescript
const x = 2;
const y = x + 2;
(() => @{
  const x = 3;
  return x * y;
@})();
// => 12
@end example

@noindent
will have the value 12 because @code{y} is computed from the outer @code{x},
so it will be 4, while inside the body of the block @code{x}
will be 3.  Had the block declared @code{const x = 3} and only then read
@code{x} in another initializer of the same block, the host would raise an
error instead: a @code{const} binding exists from the top of its scope, but
reading it before its declaration line is a temporal-dead-zone error.  Computing
the initializers before the block opens, as here, sidesteps the trap.

@end itemize

@noindent
Sometimes we can use nested definitions to get the same effect as with a
binding block, as the @code{fHelper} version of @code{fxy} above does.  We
prefer, however, to use a block in situations like this and to use nested
definitions only for internal procedures.@footnote{Understanding
internal definitions well enough to be sure a program means what we intend it
to mean requires a more elaborate model of the evaluation process than we have
presented in this chapter.  The subtleties do not arise with internal
definitions of procedures, however.  We will return to this issue in
@ref{4.1.6}, after we learn more about evaluation.}

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 1.34}Exercise 1.34:} Suppose we define the procedure

@example typescript
const f = (g: (n: number) => number): number => g(2);
@end example

@noindent
Then we have

@example typescript
f(square);
// => 4

f((z: number): number => z * (z + 1));
// => 6
@end example

@noindent
What happens if we (perversely) ask the host to evaluate the combination
@code{f(f)}?  In the interpreter of @ref{1.1.5} the question is whether
applying the number 2 as a procedure can ever happen; in TypeScript the
question never reaches the interpreter at all.  Explain why the program does
not type check, and what the type checker complains about.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 1.34a}Exercise 1.34a:} @emph{Exercise 1.34a is
added by this edition and extends exercise 1.34; SICP numbers stop at
1.34.}  Exercise 1.34 stops at the type checker's refusal, so the dynamic half
of the question goes unasked.  Ask both halves of the same program.  First,
write the typed @code{f} of @ref{Exercise 1.34} together with the call
@code{f(f)}, run the host's type checker on the file, and quote the diagnostic
it prints in the rationale.  Second, erase the types: give @code{f} the host's
escape hatch, the @code{any} type, so that the checker stands down, and run the
program, catching the error the host raises when the call finally executes.
Pin the kind of error and its message, and explain which application turned the
number 2 into the callee.  The lesson of the pair is that the type checker
refuses statically exactly what the host would fail on at runtime.
@end quotation

@node	1.3.3, 1.3.4, 1.3.2, 1.3
@subsection Procedures as General Methods

We introduced compound procedures in @ref{1.1.4} as a mechanism for
abstracting patterns of numerical operations so as to make them independent of
the particular numbers involved.  With higher-order procedures, such as the
@code{integral} procedure of @ref{1.3.1}, we began to see a more
powerful kind of abstraction: procedures used to express general methods of
computation, independent of the particular functions involved.  In this section
we discuss two more elaborate examples---general methods for finding zeros and
fixed points of functions---and show how these methods can be expressed
directly as procedures.

@subsubheading Finding roots of equations by the half-interval method

The @newterm{half-interval method} is a simple but powerful technique for
finding roots of an equation @math{{f(x) = 0}}, where @math{f} is a continuous
function.  The idea is that, if we are given points @math{a} and @math{b} such that
@math{{f(a) < 0 < f(b)}}, then @math{f} must have at least one zero between
@math{a} and @math{b}.  To locate a zero, let @math{x} be the average of @math{a} and @math{b},
and compute @math{{f(x)}}.  If @math{{f(x) > 0}}, then @math{f} must have a zero
between @math{a} and @math{x}.  If @math{{f(x) < 0}}, then @math{f} must have a zero
between @math{x} and @math{b}.  Continuing in this way, we can identify smaller and
smaller intervals on which @math{f} must have a zero.  When we reach a point where
the interval is small enough, the process stops.  Since the interval of
uncertainty is reduced by half at each step of the process, the number of steps
required grows as @math{{\Theta(\log(L\, /\, T))}}, where @math{L} is the
length of the original interval and @math{T} is the error tolerance (that is, the
size of the interval we will consider ``small enough'').  Here is a procedure
that implements this strategy:

@example typescript
const search = (
  f: (x: number) => number,
  negPoint: number,
  posPoint: number,
): number => @{
  const midpoint = average(negPoint, posPoint);
  if (closeEnough(negPoint, posPoint)) @{
    return midpoint;
  @}
  const testValue = f(midpoint);
  if (testValue > 0) @{
    return search(f, negPoint, midpoint);
  @}
  if (testValue < 0) @{
    return search(f, midpoint, posPoint);
  @}
  return midpoint;
@};
@end example

@noindent
We assume that we are initially given the function @math{f} together with points
at which its values are negative and positive.  We first compute the midpoint
of the two given points.  Next we check to see if the given interval is small
enough, and if so we simply return the midpoint as our answer.  Otherwise, we
compute as a test value the value of @math{f} at the midpoint.  If the test value
is positive, then we continue the process with a new interval running from the
original negative point to the midpoint.  If the test value is negative, we
continue with the interval from the midpoint to the positive point.  Finally,
there is the possibility that the test value is 0, in which case the midpoint
is itself the root we are searching for.  The recursion is safe here: each pass
halves the interval, so the depth is the @math{{\Theta(\log(L / T))}} of the method
itself.

To test whether the endpoints are ``close enough'' we can use a procedure
similar to the one used in @ref{1.1.7} for computing square
roots:@footnote{We have used 0.001 as a representative ``small'' number to
indicate a tolerance for the acceptable error in a calculation.  The
appropriate tolerance for a real calculation depends upon the problem to be
solved and the limitations of the computer and the algorithm.  This is often a
very subtle consideration, requiring help from a numerical analyst or some
other kind of magician.}

@example typescript
const closeEnough = (x: number, y: number): boolean =>
  abs(x - y) < 0.001;
@end example

@noindent
@code{Search} is awkward to use directly, because we can accidentally give it
points at which @math{f}'s values do not have the required sign, in which case we
get a wrong answer.  Instead we will use @code{search} via the following
procedure, which checks to see which of the endpoints has a negative function
value and which has a positive value, and calls the @code{search} procedure
accordingly.  If the function has the same sign on the two given points, the
half-interval method cannot be used, in which case the procedure signals an
error.@footnote{This can be accomplished by returning a domain error as a
value, the way @ref{0.6} introduced.}

@example typescript
type HalfIntervalResult =
  | @{ readonly _tag: "Ok"; readonly value: number @}
  | @{
      readonly _tag: "SameSign";
      readonly a: number;
      readonly b: number;
    @};

const halfIntervalMethod = (
  f: (x: number) => number,
  a: number,
  b: number,
): HalfIntervalResult => @{
  const aValue = f(a);
  const bValue = f(b);
  if (aValue < 0 && bValue > 0) @{
    return @{ _tag: "Ok", value: search(f, a, b) @};
  @}
  if (bValue < 0 && aValue > 0) @{
    return @{ _tag: "Ok", value: search(f, b, a) @};
  @}
  return @{ _tag: "SameSign", a, b @};
@};
@end example

@noindent
The following example uses the half-interval method to approximate @math{\pi} as
the root between 2 and 4 of @math{{\sin x = 0}}:

@example typescript
halfIntervalMethod(Math.sin, 2.0, 4.0);
// => @{ _tag: "Ok", value: 3.14111328125 @}
@end example

@noindent
Here is another example, using the half-interval method to search for a root of
the equation @math{{x^3 - 2x - 3 = 0}} between 1 and 2:

@example typescript
halfIntervalMethod((x) => cube(x) - 2 * x - 3, 1.0, 2.0);
// => @{ _tag: "Ok", value: 1.89306640625 @}
@end example

@subsubheading Finding fixed points of functions

A number @math{x} is called a @newterm{fixed point} of a function @math{f} if @math{x}
satisfies the equation @math{{f(x) = x}}.  For some functions @math{f} we can
locate a fixed point by beginning with an initial guess and applying @math{f}
repeatedly,
@displaymath
{f(x),}\quad {f(f(x)),}\quad {f(f(f(x))),} \quad{\dots,}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
f(x), f(f(x)), f(f(f(x))), ...
@end example

@end ifinfo
@noindent
until the value does not change very much.  Using this idea, we can devise a
procedure @code{fixedPoint} that takes as inputs a function and an initial
guess and produces an approximation to a fixed point of the function.  We apply
the function repeatedly until we find two successive values whose difference is
less than some prescribed tolerance.  Node gives no tail-call guarantee, so the
repeatedly-improving guess runs as a loop over the state:

@example typescript
const TOLERANCE = 0.00001;

const fixedPoint = (
  f: (x: number) => number,
  firstGuess: number,
): number => @{
  let guess = firstGuess;
  for (;;) @{
    const next = f(guess);
    if (abs(guess - next) < TOLERANCE) @{
      return next;
    @}
    guess = next;
  @}
@};
@end example

@noindent
For example, we can use this method to approximate the fixed point of the
cosine function, starting with 1 as an initial approximation:@footnote{Try this
during a boring lecture: Set your calculator to radians mode and then
repeatedly press the @code{cos} button until you obtain the fixed point.}

@example typescript
fixedPoint(Math.cos, 1.0);
// => 0.7390822985224023
@end example

@noindent
Similarly, we can find a solution to the equation
@math{{y = \sin y + \cos y}}:

@example typescript
fixedPoint((y) => Math.sin(y) + Math.cos(y), 1.0);
// => 1.2587315962971173
@end example

@noindent
The fixed-point process is reminiscent of the process we used for finding
square roots in @ref{1.1.7}.  Both are based on the idea of repeatedly
improving a guess until the result satisfies some criterion.  In fact, we can
readily formulate the square-root computation as a fixed-point search.
Computing the square root of some number @math{x} requires finding a @math{y} such
that @math{{y^2 = x}}.  Putting this equation into the equivalent form
@math{{y = x / y}}, we recognize that we are looking for a fixed point of the
function@footnote{@math{\mapsto} (pronounced ``maps to'') is the mathematician's way of
writing an arrow function.  @math{{y \mapsto x / y}} means @code{(y) => x / y},
that is, the function whose value at @math{y} is @math{{x / y}}.} @math{{y \mapsto x / y}},
and we can therefore try to compute square roots as

@example typescript
const sqrtByFixedPointNaive = (x: number): number =>
  fixedPoint((y) => x / y, 1.0);
@end example

@noindent
Unfortunately, this fixed-point search does not converge.  Consider an initial
guess @math{y_1}.  The next guess is @math{{y_2 = x / y_1}} and the next guess is
@math{y_3 = {x / y_2} = {x / (x / y_1)} = y_1}.  This results in an
infinite loop in which the two guesses @math{y_1} and @math{y_2}
repeat over and over, oscillating about the answer.

One way to control such oscillations is to prevent the guesses from changing so
much.  Since the answer is always between our guess @math{y} and @math{{x / y}}, we
can make a new guess that is not as far from @math{y} as @math{{x / y}} by averaging
@math{y} with @math{{x / y}}, so that the next guess after @math{y} is
@math{{{1\over2}(y + x / y)}}
instead of @math{{x / y}}.  The process of making such a sequence of
guesses is simply the process of looking for a fixed point of
@math{y \mapsto {{1\over2}(y + x / y)}}:

@example typescript
const sqrtByAverageDamp = (x: number): number =>
  fixedPoint((y) => average(y, x / y), 1.0);

sqrtByAverageDamp(2.0);
// => 1.4142135623746899
@end example

@noindent
(Note that @math{y = {{1\over2}(y + x / y)}} is a simple transformation of the
equation @math{{y = x / y;}} to derive it, add @math{y} to both sides of the
equation and divide by 2.)

With this modification, the square-root procedure works.  In fact, if we
unravel the definitions, we can see that the sequence of approximations to the
square root generated here is precisely the same as the one generated by our
original square-root procedure of @ref{1.1.7}.  This approach of
averaging successive approximations to a solution, a technique that we call
@newterm{average damping}, often aids the convergence of fixed-point searches.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 1.35}Exercise 1.35:} Show that the golden ratio
@math{\varphi} (@ref{1.2.2}) is a fixed point of the transformation
@math{{x \mapsto 1 + 1 / x}}, and use this fact to compute @math{\varphi} by means
of the @code{fixedPoint} procedure.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 1.36}Exercise 1.36:} Modify @code{fixedPoint} so that
it reports the sequence of approximations it generates, handing each new guess
to an @code{emit} callback (the host's @code{console.log} can serve at the call
site, the way @code{display} and @code{newline} served in @ref{Exercise 1.22}).
Then find a solution to @math{{x^x = 1000}} by finding a fixed point of @math{x \mapsto
{\log(1000) / \log(x)}}.  (The host's @code{Math.log}
procedure computes natural logarithms.)  Compare the number of steps
this takes with and without average damping.  (Note that you cannot start
the search at 1, as this would cause division by
@math{{\log(1) = 0}}.)
@end quotation

@quotation
